// error: what is passed for `value` of `Mutex` is isolated, so nothing it runs may reach module-level mutable state, but through `make` it reaches `made`, a module-level `var`, which every thread would share; share it through an `Atomic` or a `Mutex` held in a `val`
// error: what is passed for `body` of `Mutex.with` is isolated, so nothing it runs may reach module-level mutable state, but through `remember` it reaches `last`, a module-level `var`, which every thread would share; share it through an `Atomic` or a `Mutex` held in a `val`
// error: what is passed for `body` of `Mutex.with` is isolated, so nothing it runs may reach module-level mutable state, but it reaches `count`, a module-level `var`, which every thread would share; share it through an `Atomic` or a `Mutex` held in a `val`
// error: what is passed for `body` of `Mutex.with` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `b`, a `Buffer[int]`, which is mutable; an immutable copy (`toVector`) is sendable
// error: what is passed for `body` of `Mutex.with` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `f`, a `(Buffer[int]) -> int`, which is a function value, whose type does not say what it captures; mark the parameter `@isolating`, and what is passed for it is checked in turn
// More a mutex's value and body may not do: a value made by a function that keeps it in a
// module-level `var` too, a body handing the value to a generic function that keeps it, and a body
// that only reads a module-level `var` (the price of the rule: read it into a local first). A nested
// `with` on another mutex may not capture the outer value, and an `@isolated` parameter is too weak
// to pass on for `with`'s body.
import avin.threads.Mutex

var made = Buffer[int]()
var last: Any = 0
var count = 0
val other = Mutex(Buffer[Buffer[int]]())

make() -> Buffer[int]
    val b = Buffer[int]()
    made = b
    b

remember[T](x: T) = last = x

apply(m: Mutex[Buffer[int]], @isolated f: (Buffer[int]) -> int) -> int = m.with(f)

main()
    val m = Mutex(make())
    m.with(b -> remember(b))
    m.with(b -> b.addOne(count))
    m.with(b -> other.with(bb -> bb.addOne(b)))
    println(apply(m, b -> b.length()))
